╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ swap_neighborhood_search                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ Add a direct transaction-swap neighborhood to the existing insertion local search.        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Insertion moves are strong, but they cannot represent every useful ordering change in  │
│                          │ one step. In particular, swapping two transactions separated by other transactions        │
│                          │ changes only the relative order of that pair, whereas moving one transaction also shifts  │
│                          │ every transaction in between.                                                             │
│                          │ 2. For read/write-conflict schedules, a pair of transactions can form a conflict barrier: │
│                          │ exchanging only those two may remove a critical-path dependency without perturbing        │
│                          │ beneficial ordering among the intervening transactions.                                   │
│                          │ 3. The new search evaluates direct swaps using `get_opt_seq_cost`, exactly as the rest of │
│                          │ the scheduler does. Therefore it optimizes the true conflict-aware makespan rather than a │
│                          │ heuristic.                                                                                │
│                          │ 4. The swap candidates compete with insertion candidates in the same best-improvement     │
│                          │ round. If a swap reaches a new basin of the search space, later rounds of insertion       │
│                          │ search refine it further.                                                                 │
│                          │ 5. For large workloads, the code reuses the existing structured position sample,          │
│                          │ preserving the current bounded-search behavior.                                           │
