│ patch_name               │ segment_reversal_search                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ Add a reversal-neighborhood phase to the existing local search.                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. The current optimizer evaluates insertions and arbitrary exchanges. Those moves are    │
│                          │ effective for moving one or two transactions, but they can be trapped when a whole        │
│                          │ consecutive region has the wrong conflict direction.                                      │
│                          │ 2. A segment reversal changes the relative order of every pair inside a contiguous block  │
│                          │ in one move. This can simultaneously shorten multiple read/write and write/write          │
│                          │ dependency chains, which is particularly useful when beam search selected a promising set │
│                          │ of transactions but arranged a conflicting subsequence poorly.                            │
│                          │ 3. The reversal is evaluated with `workload.get_opt_seq_cost`, exactly like all existing  │
│                          │ moves. Therefore acceptance remains based on the true conflict-aware makespan rather than │
│                          │ a proxy heuristic.                                                                        │
│                          │ 4. Length-two reversals are omitted because they are already covered by the existing swap │
│                          │ neighborhood. Larger reversals add genuinely new candidate schedules.                     │
│                          │ 5. The existing structured position subset is retained for large workloads, so this       │
│                          │ additional neighborhood follows the same scalability policy as insertion and swap search. │
