│ patch_name               │ impact_guided_reversal_search                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current large-workload local search samples positions uniformly. Uniform spacing can  │
│                          │ miss the transactions responsible for the dominant conflict chain, so it can spend        │
│                          │ evaluations on moves that cannot reduce the simulator’s actual makespan.                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This edit restores objective-guided selection for large schedules: temporarily remove     │
│                          │ each transaction and measure its marginal impact with `get_opt_seq_cost`. Transactions    │
│                          │ with the largest measured impact, together with evenly spaced anchors, become candidate   │
│                          │ move sources and nearby destinations. This focuses insertion and exchange search on       │
│                          │ conflict bottlenecks without using operation-count proxies.                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ It also adds interval-reversal moves to local descent. A reversal changes many precedence │
│                          │ relationships simultaneously, allowing descent to escape schedules where every single     │
│                          │ insertion and pair exchange is non-improving. Reversals are evaluated with the same exact │
│                          │ simulator objective and are restricted to the already selected positions for large        │
│                          │ workloads, keeping the search bounded and deterministic.                                  │
